{
 "_doc": "Crew leveling (change crew-leveling, mockup 39; owner, 2026-11-12: \"ensigns provide no bonus but gain xp overtime, and can be traded for prestige so you can get more experienced crew, or just level up your crew member\", \"need crew skill tree\", \"skill tree, each level gets a skill point, the player can auto level officers or pick paths on the tree to go down and specialize\"). After the Starfleet Command 3 manual's officers (printed page 36: officers gain experience over a campaign, their worth and skills rising, and are swapped for more experienced ones from a pool for prestige; three skills to a station), in our own words and numbers. ranks: rank names, the first an ensign; an officer has as many skill points as their rank's index, so an ensign has none and brings nothing. xp_next: experience to reach the next rank from each rank, spent on the promotion. xp_battle: experience to every officer of the crew for a battle fought, times grade_x by the mission's grade. xp_turn: experience to every officer for each campaign turn served (the time aboard). xp_per_prestige: the rate between experience and prestige, both ways: an officer transferred out is worth their rank's worth plus their banked experience at this rate, and a promotion's shortfall may be paid in prestige at it. worth: what an officer of each rank is worth in prestige when transferred out. hire: what one of each rank costs to hire from the pool, in prestige. retrain: the prestige to take back every point an officer has placed. pool: how many of each rank are on offer at each station. trees: for each station, three branches of three tiers; a node is taken with one point, a tier only once the tier above it in its branch is taken; each node's effect is a change to an ability read by its battle rule (ability: the key the rule reads, value per node; a capstone may name its own rule). auto: the order an officer on auto level spends their points in, one station's own path.",
